---
// Lesson 10.2d — Turn Logic: Selection + Iteration
// rpg jam: implement AI_Turn() (the other empty method); game loop logic.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>10.2d — Turn Logic: Selection + Iteration</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    The starter's other empty method: <code>AI_Turn()</code>. Your
    opponent's brain. Today you build an AI that reads a list, makes
    a decision, and takes its turn — the exam's algorithm
    requirements (sequencing + selection + iteration) assembled into
    one method you can point to on the exam as YOURS.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Concept Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>What does an AI turn DO?</strong> Read the starter's
    partially-implemented Deal() and your <a href="10.2c.html">10.2c</a>
    draw; the AI's turn is a decision on top of the same lists.
    Design the three layers before coding:</p>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Traverse</strong> the AI's hand (iteration)</li>
        <li><strong>Score</strong> each card by your game's rules
            (selection: which is playable? which is best?)</li>
        <li><strong>Play</strong> one: remove from hand, resolve its
            effect, end turn (sequencing)</li>
    </ol>
    <p>That is literally the exam's algorithm definition wearing a
    hat — and it is why this method is the Create PT's best friend:
    a list, a procedure with parameters, a decision loop, a call.</p>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic</h3>
        <p>
            "AI" in your game is a set of IF statements — no
            learning, no mystery. Why is that honest design for a
            card game (what does the player actually want from a
            jam opponent — cleverness, or READABLE rules)? Where
            would REAL machine learning belong instead
            (<a href="9.6a.html">9.6a</a> — and whose data would
            it learn from)?
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~35 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Step 1 — the greedy AI (20 min).</strong> Fill
    AI_Turn() in the starter's voice:</p>
    <pre><code>public void AI_Turn()
{
    Card bestCard = null;
    int bestScore = -1;

    foreach (Card c in aiHand)              // ITERATION
    {
        if (c.cost &lt;= aiEnergy)              // SELECTION: playable?
        {
            int score = c.attack * 2 + c.defense;
            if (score &gt; bestScore)           // SELECTION: best?
            {
                bestScore = score;
                bestCard = c;
            }
        }
    }

    if (bestCard != null)                   // SELECTION: can we play?
    {
        aiHand.Remove(bestCard);
        PlayCard(bestCard, owner: AI);      // your resolve procedure
    }
    else
    {
        Pass();
    }
    EndTurn();
}</code></pre>
    <p>Read it aloud: traverse, filter, compare, act, or pass. The
    null check at the end is the same guard-clause religion as the
    empty deck (<a href="10.2c.html">10.2c</a>) — an AI with
    NOTHING playable must pass, not crash.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2 — the turn loop.</strong> Whose turn is it?
    One bool (playerTurn) flipped by EndTurn(), with player input
    gated behind it — the turret's cooldown pattern
    (<a href="3.3a.html">3.3a</a>) repurposed as a STATE GATE.
    Trace one full round on paper: player draws (Deal), plays,
    ends turn → bool flips → AI_Turn() runs → bool flips back.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 3 — a 1.0s pause for the AI turn.</strong> The
    AI playing instantly feels like a bug to players. A simple
    coroutine or a timer (<a href="3.3a.html">3.3a</a> timestamps!)
    before AI_Turn() runs gives the game the rhythm of an
    opponent. Feel is not decoration — it is legibility.</p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: AI bugs</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>The AI plays its best card EVERY turn and always
                wins. Your game has a balance bug or an AI bug —
                which, and what single number would you change to
                test which one it is?</li>
            <li>The AI plays TWO cards in one turn. Where did the
                gate fail — and why is "one action per turn" a
                STATE question, not an IF question?</li>
            <li>Best card score ties (two cards score 9). Which
                does your foreach pick, and why is
                "first found wins" actually FINE (what does
                determinism buy a tester)?</li>
            <li>The player's turn triggers while AI_Turn() is
                still resolving. Draw the timeline of the bool
                flips — which flip came EARLY, and which line
                must move?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Close of Day</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~5 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: AI personality</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Give the AI a PERSONALITY by changing the score
                line: aggressive (attack × 3), defensive
                (defense × 3 + heal bonus). Play each — can a
                tester tell the difference blind? That is your
                <a href="9.5a.html">9.5a</a> experiment design
                muscle, in a jam.</li>
            <li>Commit + push: <code>"10.2d: AI_Turn implemented + turn gate"</code>.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Implement a greedy AI: traverse, filter, compare, act/pass</li>
            <li>Explain the turn loop as a bool state gate</li>
            <li>Label sequencing/selection/iteration inside AI_Turn on sight</li>
            <li>Give an AI personality via one score line</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-10/lists-arrays.html">Lists &amp; Arrays</a> — traversals and filters</li>
        <li><a href="../exam/create-performance-task.html">Create PT</a> — this method's second home</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="10.2e.html">10.2e — Card Jam: Build + Playtest</a></p>
</div>`;
